From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 11:41:56 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] Video as a hardware overlay of its own, under the browser's
 layer

Fullscreen 1080p60 cost the compositor 22-29 ms a frame with no overdraw to
speak of: the cost was the video, sampled from NV12 and scaled to 2560x1440
by the 3D engine. The display can do that itself -- NV12 overlays with
scaling -- and an NV12 swap chain given to a XAML panel was shown as a
hardware overlay in every test, including under a translucent browser layer.

When WebRender is about to composite an opaque NV12 video surface it offers
the frame to GeckoW10mVideoLayer first. The frame is copied into an NV12
swap chain on the compositor's device whose composition surface the shell
puts in a panel under the browser's; the shell is told where the video is
and acknowledges each placement; once the placement this frame needs has
been acknowledged and the video has stood still for a frame, WebRender draws
a transparent hole instead of the video (blending is off for opaque tiles),
and what lies over the video is blended over the hole as before. Whenever
the video moves the hole closes until the panel has caught up; when the
video is gone for two presented frames the panel is hidden. Anything
refused turns the layer off for the run and WebRender keeps drawing videos.
gfx.webrender.gecko-w10m.video-overlay = false turns it off.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
